  {
    "id": "FORMAT-TZX-CONTROL-INFO-001",
    "tier": "contract",
    "provenance": "contract",
    "input": {
      "kind": "tzx-format-query",
      "note": "TZX v1.20 control blocks (FMT-TZX-CONTROL-001: 0x23 jump, 0x24/0x25 loop, 0x26/0x27 call/return, 0x28 select, 0x2A stop-if-48K, 0x2B signal level) and informational blocks (FMT-TZX-INFO-001: 0x31 message, 0x32 archive info, 0x33 hardware type, 0x35 custom info, 0x5A glue). The file is laid out by hand from the spec block tables: 0x32 archive info: WORD 11 = rest of block; 2 entries: (type 00 \"Demo\") (type 02 \"ZX\"); 0x33 hardware type: 1 entry (type 00, id 00, info 01); 0x31 message: 5 s, \"Hi\"; 0x35 custom info: 16-character ident \"POKEs\" + 11 spaces (the spec writes CHAR[10] in hex), DWORD 3, 3 bytes; 0x5A glue: \"XTape!\" 1A 01 14; 0x24 loop start: 3 repetitions; 0x12 pure tone inside the loop: 2168 T x 2 pulses; 0x25 loop end; 0x2B set signal level: DWORD 1, level 1 (high); 0x2A stop the tape if in 48K mode: DWORD 0; 0x23 jump to block: -1; 0x26 call sequence: 2 calls, +1 and -2; 0x27 return from sequence; 0x28 select block: WORD 10 = rest; 2 entries: (+1 \"A\") (+2 \"BC\"). Real-world .tzx files nearly always carry these; the pre-2026-11 parser rejected every one of them. The parser returns them as blocks (never silently dropped); none has a `data` field, so a consumer that loads `data` blocks (the instant loader) skips them, while a signal player must honour the control blocks.",
      "file": "5a5854617065211a0114320b0002000444656d6f02025a583301000001310502486935504f4b45732020202020202020202020030000000102035a5854617065211a01142403001278080200252b01000000012a0000000023ffff2602000100feff27280a0002010001410200024243",
